declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-101';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 14, $j$4.2 The Weeping Prophet: Jeremiah and Lamentations$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Jeremiah 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Jeremiah/1"}, {"title": "Jeremiah 29 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Jeremiah/29"}, {"title": "Lamentations 3 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Lamentations/3"}], "body": "Read Jeremiah 1, Jeremiah 29 and Lamentations 3 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nJeremiah was called to be a prophet as a young man, about six hundred years before Christ. For more than forty years he warned Judah that Babylon would take the city if the people did not repent. They did not listen, and he was mocked, beaten and imprisoned. He is often called the weeping prophet. When Babylon destroyed Jerusalem, Jeremiah grieved over the city. Lamentations is a set of five poems of sorrow, traditionally credited to Jeremiah, that mourn the fall of Jerusalem while still holding on to hope in the faithfulness of God. Jeremiah also wrote to the exiles in Babylon and told them God had plans for their future.\n\nKey words: calling, repentance, exile, lament, new covenant.\n\nYour task: (1) Say what God told young Jeremiah in chapter 1. (2) Tell what Jeremiah told the exiles to do in Babylon. (3) Write Lamentations 3:22 and 23 in your own words.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Jeremiah was a young man when God called him. He said, Ah, Lord GOD! behold, I cannot speak: for I am a child. God answered, Say not, I am a child: for thou shalt go to all that I shall send thee, and whatsoever I command thee thou shalt speak. God did not ask Jeremiah to be strong on his own. He promised to be with him.\n\nJeremiah served for more than forty years during the last days of the kingdom of Judah, about six hundred years before Christ. His message was unpopular. He told the people that if they did not repent, Babylon would conquer Jerusalem. The kings and the false prophets said everything was fine, but Jeremiah told the truth. For that he was mocked, beaten, thrown into a cistern and put in prison. Because of his sorrow over his people he is often called the weeping prophet.\n\nJeremiah was right. In the days of King Zedekiah, Babylon took Jerusalem, burned the temple and carried many of the people into exile. The book of Lamentations was written in the aftermath. It is a set of five poems that grieve over the destroyed city. Yet right in the middle of the sorrow there is a light. Lamentations 3:22 and 23 say, It is of the LORD's mercies that we are not consumed, because his compassions fail not. They are new every morning: great is thy faithfulness.\n\nJeremiah also wrote a letter to the exiles in Babylon. He told them not to give up, but to build houses, plant gardens, and pray for the city where they lived. Then he gave them a promise. Jeremiah 29:11 says, For I know the thoughts that I think toward you, saith the LORD, thoughts of peace, and not of evil, to give you an expected end. And the people were told in verse 13, And ye shall seek me, and find me, when ye shall search for me with all your heart.\n\nOne of the great promises in the book is in Jeremiah 31. God said He would make a new covenant with His people, unlike the one made at Sinai. He would write His law in their hearts. Christians understand that this new covenant is fulfilled through Jesus Christ.\n\nYour task is to say what God told Jeremiah in chapter 1, tell what he told the exiles to do, and put Lamentations 3:22 and 23 in your own words. Then take the four question check.\n\nGod's mercies are new every morning, even in the hardest days. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$4.2 The Weeping Prophet: Jeremiah and Lamentations$j$);
end
$seed$;
